Date: Tue, 03 May 2011 20:38:56 -0400
Subject: Impute missing data on survey data which has zero weight?
Message-ID: <4DC0A020.90908@gmail.com>

Hi All:

I have a basic question about multiple imputation. I have a survey 
dataset (16000 cases) with  950 cases having zero weight. Do I need to 
keep those cases in my dataset or drop them before MI? Thanks!

Li Chang
2012 M.A. Candidate
Economics and Education
Teachers College, Columbia University

From king-nRXb03v5zC6Vc3sceRu5cw at public.gmane.org  Wed May  4 01:05:53 2011
From: king-nRXb03v5zC6Vc3sceRu5cw at public.gmane.org (Gary King)
Date: Tue, 03 May 2011 21:05:53 -0400
Subject: Re: Impute missing data on survey data which has zero weight?
In-Reply-To: <4DC0A020.90908@gmail.com>
References: <4DC0A020.90908@gmail.com>
Message-ID: <BANLkTinqUEL2aL7mOVmu0YB8wJ37CGFigg@mail.gmail.com>

the weights, however, are not important for imputation.  the question is
whether the extra observations contain information that might help you
predict the missing values in the data without zero weights.  if it does,
the you might keep it.  they won't have a direct effect on your analysis
model but might in this way have an effect on improving the imputations.
Gary
